WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.867
A California city launches an online rebate application.

2
00:00:02.867 --> 00:00:07.832
Many homeowners finish it, but renters and residents using mobile phones leave before submitting.

3
00:00:07.832 --> 00:00:14.743
Is that difference about personal motivation, or does the application interact with culture, groups, organizations, and social control?

4
00:00:14.743 --> 00:00:17.129
This episode is for study preparation only.

5
00:00:17.129 --> 00:00:24.009
It is not official exam preparation, accredited instruction, or a claim that listening will earn or transfer college credit.

6
00:00:24.009 --> 00:00:28.281
Any credit decision requires written approval from the receiving institution.

7
00:00:28.281 --> 00:00:33.625
Sociology is the systematic study of society, social interaction, and patterns of group life.

8
00:00:33.625 --> 00:00:39.543
The sociological imagination connects personal experiences with wider social and historical conditions.

9
00:00:39.543 --> 00:00:42.416
One unfinished application is an individual event.

10
00:00:42.416 --> 00:00:49.139
A repeated drop at the same question, concentrated among people in similar situations, may point to a social pattern.

11
00:00:49.139 --> 00:00:51.323
Sociologists work at different levels.

12
00:00:51.323 --> 00:00:56.207
Micro-level analysis examines face-to-face interaction, identities, and small groups.

13
00:00:56.207 --> 00:01:00.861
Macro-level analysis examines institutions, inequality, and large social systems.

14
00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:03.102
A city service can be viewed both ways.

15
00:01:03.102 --> 00:01:05.573
A confusing exchange with a clerk is micro.

16
00:01:05.573 --> 00:01:08.619
A rule that affects thousands of applicants is macro.

17
00:01:08.619 --> 00:01:09.998
The two levels interact.

18
00:01:09.998 --> 00:01:14.250
Three broad theoretical perspectives organize many sociological questions.

19
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:21.202
Structural functionalism examines how parts of society contribute to stability and how disruptions or dysfunctions arise.

20
00:01:21.202 --> 00:01:25.684
A standardized application can coordinate work and produce comparable records.

21
00:01:25.684 --> 00:01:29.304
Its dysfunction may be that eligible people cannot complete it.

22
00:01:29.304 --> 00:01:32.982
Conflict theory emphasizes competition over power and resources.

23
00:01:32.982 --> 00:01:38.441
It asks who designs the rule, whose knowledge counts, and who receives benefits or bears costs.

24
00:01:38.441 --> 00:01:46.658
If a rebate requires the applicant to own the property, landlords and renters occupy different positions even when both want lower utility use.

25
00:01:46.658 --> 00:01:50.795
Symbolic interactionism focuses on meanings created through interaction.

26
00:01:50.795 --> 00:01:58.265
Labels such as incomplete, suspicious, or noncompliant can shape how staff understand an applicant and how the applicant responds.

27
00:01:58.265 --> 00:02:02.804
A government seal may signal trust to one resident and surveillance to another.

28
00:02:02.804 --> 00:02:05.677
The perspectives do not provide automatic answers.

29
00:02:05.677 --> 00:02:08.205
They direct attention to different evidence.

30
00:02:08.205 --> 00:02:11.193
Sociology uses research rather than intuition alone.

31
00:02:11.193 --> 00:02:22.456
A researcher begins with a question, reviews existing knowledge, develops a hypothesis when appropriate, defines concepts, selects a method, gathers evidence, analyzes results, and reports limits.

32
00:02:22.456 --> 00:02:26.306
Reliability means a measure produces reasonably consistent results.

33
00:02:26.306 --> 00:02:30.155
Validity asks whether it measures the concept it claims to measure.

34
00:02:30.155 --> 00:02:32.282
Ethics matter throughout the process.

35
00:02:32.282 --> 00:02:42.797
Researchers should reduce harm, protect privacy and confidentiality, seek informed consent when required, and avoid misleading participants without strong justification and oversight.

36
00:02:42.797 --> 00:02:45.095
Official city data deserve similar care.

37
00:02:45.095 --> 00:02:48.945
A detailed dataset can expose identities even if names are removed.

38
00:02:48.945 --> 00:02:51.588
Correlation means two variables vary together.

39
00:02:51.588 --> 00:02:54.634
It does not by itself show that one causes the other.

40
00:02:54.634 --> 00:02:59.748
Mobile users may abandon the rebate form more often, but the device may not be the cause.

41
00:02:59.748 --> 00:03:06.356
Mobile use could be associated with work schedule, income, internet access, age, or the urgency of the application.

42
00:03:06.356 --> 00:03:08.252
A sound study tests alternatives.

43
00:03:08.252 --> 00:03:14.156
Culture includes a society's shared practices, values, beliefs, symbols, language, and material objects.

44
00:03:14.156 --> 00:03:17.505
Material culture includes physical things and technologies.

45
00:03:17.505 --> 00:03:20.741
Nonmaterial culture includes meanings, norms, and values.

46
00:03:20.741 --> 00:03:22.614
A smartphone is material culture.

47
00:03:22.614 --> 00:03:28.575
Expectations about whether government should be trusted with personal data belong to nonmaterial culture.

48
00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:29.654
Culture is learned.

49
00:03:29.654 --> 00:03:33.741
A subculture shares features that distinguish it within a wider culture.

50
00:03:33.741 --> 00:03:37.261
Countercultures reject important elements of dominant culture.

51
00:03:37.261 --> 00:03:43.051
Cultural relativism means evaluating a culture in its own context before applying outside assumptions.

52
00:03:43.051 --> 00:03:46.400
Ethnocentrism judges other cultures by one's own standards.

53
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:54.007
For civic technology, this matters when designers treat one language, household structure, or communication style as naturally normal.

54
00:03:54.007 --> 00:03:58.719
Socialization is the lifelong process of learning culture, roles, and expectations.

55
00:03:58.719 --> 00:04:01.103
Family is an early agent of socialization.

56
00:04:01.103 --> 00:04:04.282
Schools teach formal material and informal expectations.

57
00:04:04.282 --> 00:04:08.313
Peers, workplaces, religion, government, and media also shape behavior.

58
00:04:08.313 --> 00:04:11.549
Socialization does not turn people into identical copies.

59
00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:17.623
It equips people to participate in social worlds while they interpret and sometimes resist what they learn.

60
00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:21.767
Roles are expected behaviors connected with a status, or social position.

61
00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:27.728
A person may hold several statuses at once, such as renter, parent, employee, and neighborhood volunteer.

62
00:04:27.728 --> 00:04:31.758
Role conflict occurs when expectations from different statuses compete.

63
00:04:31.758 --> 00:04:36.186
Role strain occurs when expectations within one role become hard to reconcile.

64
00:04:36.186 --> 00:04:42.942
An hourly worker asked to call the city during work hours may experience conflict between employee and applicant roles.

65
00:04:42.942 --> 00:04:44.872
Groups shape conduct and identity.

66
00:04:44.872 --> 00:04:48.505
A primary group is small, personal, and emotionally significant.

67
00:04:48.505 --> 00:04:51.287
A secondary group is larger or more task-focused.

68
00:04:51.287 --> 00:04:56.339
An in-group is one a person identifies with, while an out-group is one viewed as outside.

69
00:04:56.339 --> 00:05:00.370
A reference group supplies standards people use to evaluate themselves.

70
00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:04.343
Group size, leadership, conformity, and networks all affect decisions.

71
00:05:04.343 --> 00:05:07.806
Formal organizations pursue goals through planned structures.

72
00:05:07.806 --> 00:05:12.972
Bureaucracies rely on specialization, hierarchy, written rules, impersonality, and records.

73
00:05:12.972 --> 00:05:16.832
These features can make decisions predictable and reduce favoritism.

74
00:05:16.832 --> 00:05:22.963
They can also produce red tape, goal displacement, and an iron cage of rules that become ends in themselves.

75
00:05:22.963 --> 00:05:30.400
Informal practices still grow inside formal organizations, sometimes helping work move and sometimes making treatment inconsistent.

76
00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.949
Deviance is behavior or an attribute that violates social norms.

77
00:05:33.949 --> 00:05:38.774
It is socially defined, so what counts as deviant varies across time, place, and group.

78
00:05:38.774 --> 00:05:48.535
Social control is the regulation and enforcement of norms through internal beliefs, informal reactions, and formal institutions such as schools, workplaces, police, and courts.

79
00:05:48.535 --> 00:05:52.472
Functionalist approaches ask what deviance does within a social system.

80
00:05:52.472 --> 00:05:56.687
It can clarify boundaries, strengthen group solidarity, or encourage change.

81
00:05:56.687 --> 00:06:02.455
Strain theory connects deviance to a gap between culturally approved goals and socially available means.

82
00:06:02.455 --> 00:06:06.337
Conflict approaches examine how powerful groups shape laws and labels.

83
00:06:06.337 --> 00:06:18.482
Symbolic interactionist approaches include labeling theory, which studies how being labeled can affect identity and future treatment, and differential association, which emphasizes learning behavior through interaction.

84
00:06:18.482 --> 00:06:20.700
These ideas require careful application.

85
00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:25.137
Calling an application incomplete may be an accurate administrative description.

86
00:06:25.137 --> 00:06:29.296
Trouble begins when the label becomes a claim about the person's character.

87
00:06:29.296 --> 00:06:36.838
A resident who misses a deadline may lack a document, stable internet, time away from work, or knowledge of an unwritten office routine.

88
00:06:36.838 --> 00:06:40.720
The category should describe the record, not invent a moral diagnosis.

89
00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.162
Return to the city rebate.

90
00:06:42.162 --> 00:06:43.660
Start with research design.

91
00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:46.544
Define completion and identify the unit of analysis.

92
00:06:46.544 --> 00:06:49.095
A browser session is not necessarily a person.

93
00:06:49.095 --> 00:06:52.034
A person who saves and returns is not two applicants.

94
00:06:52.034 --> 00:06:59.077
Measure where users leave, which devices they use, how long each step takes, and whether they later apply by phone or on paper.

95
00:06:59.077 --> 00:07:00.741
Then use more than one method.

96
00:07:00.741 --> 00:07:04.401
Interview people who completed the process and people who did not.

97
00:07:04.401 --> 00:07:06.564
Offer interviews in relevant languages.

98
00:07:06.564 --> 00:07:08.505
Observe staff assisting applicants.

99
00:07:08.505 --> 00:07:14.938
Review whether the form assumes property ownership, a utility account, daytime availability, or access to a scanner.

100
00:07:14.938 --> 00:07:18.154
Protect identities and explain how responses will be used.

101
00:07:18.154 --> 00:07:20.096
Use theory to sharpen the analysis.

102
00:07:20.096 --> 00:07:24.809
A functionalist question asks whether every required field serves the program's goal.

103
00:07:24.809 --> 00:07:30.244
A conflict question asks whether owners receive the benefit while renters carry the inconvenience.

104
00:07:30.244 --> 00:07:35.457
An interactionist question asks what the words fraud check or failed verification communicate.

105
00:07:35.457 --> 00:07:40.892
A cultural question asks whether translations preserve meaning rather than merely replacing words.

106
00:07:40.892 --> 00:07:42.001
Next, test a change.

107
00:07:42.001 --> 00:07:48.157
Make document upload optional until eligibility review, improve the mobile layout, or offer evening assistance.

108
00:07:48.157 --> 00:07:50.930
Compare outcomes while watching for other changes.

109
00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:56.254
If completion rises, that supports a practical improvement, but it may not prove a single cause.

110
00:07:56.254 --> 00:08:00.691
Report uncertainty instead of converting a useful result into a universal claim.

111
00:08:00.691 --> 00:08:03.574
The main lesson is not that individuals lack agency.

112
00:08:03.574 --> 00:08:06.236
Sociology studies agency within social contexts.

113
00:08:06.236 --> 00:08:14.167
Residents make choices, but they choose with unequal resources, learned expectations, group ties, organizational rules, and labels around them.

114
00:08:14.167 --> 00:08:19.879
A well-designed civic service treats those conditions as evidence, not excuses and not character flaws.

115
00:08:19.879 --> 00:08:21.666
Here are three recall questions.

116
00:08:21.666 --> 00:08:24.738
Pause after the questions and answer in your own words.

117
00:08:24.738 --> 00:08:29.318
First, how do conflict theory and symbolic interactionism ask different questions?

118
00:08:29.318 --> 00:08:32.893
Second, what is the difference between reliability and validity?

119
00:08:32.893 --> 00:08:37.529
Third, how can a bureaucratic rule support fairness and also create unequal access?

120
00:08:37.529 --> 00:08:41.894
First, conflict theory emphasizes power, resources, and which groups benefit.

121
00:08:41.894 --> 00:08:45.806
Symbolic interactionism emphasizes meanings, labels, and interaction.

122
00:08:45.806 --> 00:08:48.641
Second, reliability is consistency in measurement.

123
00:08:48.641 --> 00:08:52.666
Validity is whether the measure actually captures the intended concept.

124
00:08:52.666 --> 00:08:56.691
Third, a written rule can make similar cases receive similar treatment.

125
00:08:56.691 --> 00:09:05.365
The same rule can create unequal access if it assumes that everyone has resources such as time, language fluency, property records, or stable technology.

126
00:09:05.365 --> 00:09:11.268
For your written learner artifact, draw the rebate process from first notice to final payment.

127
00:09:11.268 --> 00:09:19.370
Mark one cultural assumption, one socialization influence, one group relationship, one bureaucratic rule, and one possible label.

128
00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:24.143
Add a research plan with one quantitative method and one qualitative method.

129
00:09:24.143 --> 00:09:31.491
Finish with two competing explanations for the completion gap and one observation that would weaken each explanation.

130
